# =============================================================================
#  runTests  --  numeric regression for Choupo tutorials
#
#  An HONEST gate -- the old `binary > /dev/null && PASS` (which passes even on
#  a NaN return, exit 0) AND its successor (which still passed a crashed run or
#  a missing result block, CLAUDE.md §10 forbidden) are both retired.  FOUR
#  checks, in order; the first to trip FAILS the case:
#
#    0. Exit-code guard (EVERY case): a non-zero exit -- a crashed or aborted
#       run, e.g. the engine refusing an unphysical state -- is a FAIL.  A
#       crash that emits no result block used to fall through to a vacuous
#       "smoke pass"; it no longer can.
#
#    1. NaN / inf guard (EVERY case): if the structured result block contains
#       a NaN or inf value, the case FAILS --- catches the silent-NaN trap a
#       plain exit-code check misses.
#
#    2. Result-block guard (cases that ship an `expected` golden): a case that
#       PROMISES golden KPIs but emits NO comparable result block FAILS -- the
#       golden cannot be checked vacuously.
#
#    3. Reference KPIs (cases that ship an `expected` file): each line names a
#       KPI or stream value, its golden-master value, and a relative
#       tolerance; the actual value is compared and the case FAILS on any
#       deviation beyond tolerance.
#
#  Two HONEST opt-outs, each loud in the summary, neither ever reported PASS:
#
#    * `.expect-nonconvergence` -- a case DELIBERATELY meant to fail as a
#       lesson (e.g. an explicit RK4 integrator blowing up on stiff chemistry,
#       ignition01_h2o2_rk4).  Counted as EXPECTED-FAIL.
#
#    * `.known-broken` -- a case that is genuinely BROKEN (a bug awaiting a
#       fix: a crash, a stale/failing golden).  The file's first line is a
#       one-line reason + a pointer to the QA report.  Counted as
#       KNOWN-BROKEN: run, reported aloud, named in the summary -- never green,
#       never a hard FAIL.  This keeps the suite green WITHOUT lying: the
#       breakage stays visible until someone fixes the physics.
#
#  EXPECTED-FAIL is a LESSON; KNOWN-BROKEN is a BUG.  They read differently on
#  purpose.
#
#  The `expected` file lives in the case directory.  Format (one per line,
#  `#` comments allowed):
#
#       # kind   name      key         expected        reltol
#       kpi      flash01   V_over_F    0.30398357      1e-4
#       stream   vapor     F           0.0084439881    1e-4
#       diag     gamma_nacl gamma_pm_1m 0.6572         1e-4
#       aad      etoh_water_1atm txy_wilson.T_bubble.aadAbs 0.275964  1e-4
#       closure  transporter step_kW    -2.98508731741 1e-4
#       utility  transporter heating.steamLP.eur_h 1.728  1e-4
#
#  `kind` names WHERE the number is read from: `kpi` (inside the "kpis" of the
#  named unit), `stream` (inside the named stream), `diag` (inside an
#  `operationResults[].diagnostics` object, `name` = the op), `aad` (inside
#  the top-level `"validation"` overlay table, `name` = the dataset and `key`
#  = `<model>.<property>.<statistic>`), `closure` (inside the top-level
#  `"energyClosures"` model-boundary ledger, `name` = the unit) or `utility`
#  (inside the top-level `"utilityAllocation"` array, `name` = the unit and
#  `key` = `<tier>.<utility>.<field>`, or `<tier>.<port>.unserved` for a duty
#  no catalogue utility can meet).  `reltol` is |actual-exp|/|exp| (falls
#  back to absolute when exp is ~0).
#
#  `anchor` is a SIXTH-COLUMN CLAIM, not a kind (2026-08-10; respelled the
#  same day -- see below).  A row is SELF-RECORDED by default: it pins that
#  the answer has not MOVED, which is regression.  A row marked `anchor`
#  carries a PUBLISHED value from the case's cited primary, so it pins that
#  the answer AGREES with a measurement -- which is validation, and the two
#  must never be confused.  Three consequences, all enforced below:
#    * `--record` COPIES an anchor row through verbatim and never refreshes
#      it.  A published number is not the model's to rewrite; a refresh
#      would silently convert a disagreement into a new "reference" -- the
#      exact laundering this claim exists to prevent.
#    * auto-generation never emits one: an anchor is AUTHORED, with its
#      source in the case, or it does not exist.
#    * a mismatch reports as ANCHOR (disagreement with the cited value),
#      not as a moved golden.
#
#       # kind   name      key         expected        reltol  claim
#       kpi      node      K_N2        3.047           3e-2    anchor
#       diag     gamma_nacl gamma_pm_1m 0.657          2e-2    anchor
#
#  WHY IT IS NOT A KIND, having been one for a day.  `kind` names WHERE the
#  number is read from; the claim names WHAT the row asserts about it.  They
#  are orthogonal, and folding them into one column bound the anchor to the
#  `kpi` lookup -- so a quantity published in `operationResults[].diagnostics`
#  (every choupoProps case, i.e. the entire Pitzer validation corpus) could
#  not be anchored at all.  The alternative was a second spelling per
#  location (`anchorDiag`, then `anchorStream`), which is the same conflation
#  paid for three times.  One column per axis; every location gets the claim
#  for free.
#
#  Written before this existed, a case's agreement with its primary lived
#  only in prose -- flash09_n2ch4_stryjek quoted Stryjek's K_N2 = 3.047 in
#  a header comment while its golden pinned F/P/T/Q, so a model that
#  drifted off the measurement would still have passed, with the header
#  still claiming agreement.  A claim in prose goes stale silently.
#
#  Usage:
#      runTests                       # every tutorial under tutorials/
#      runTests tutorials/steady/flash01_benzene_toluene   # one case
#      runTests --record <case>       # write/refresh that case's expected
#                                       from the current run (golden master)
#
#  Exit code: 0 if all pass, 1 if any fail.
# =============================================================================

set -uo pipefail

ROOT=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)

#  THE CAMPAIGN-INVALIDATING RULE (2026-08-18).  A destructive gate
#  (check_gate_selftest) sabotages sources and rebuilds the engine to prove
#  other gates can fail.  Its cleanup is try/finally, which is exception-safe
#  and NOT death-safe -- and gate_manifest kills gates with SIGKILL on
#  timeout, against which no `finally` runs.  Killed mid-sabotage it leaves a
#  POISONED BINARY, and a clean `git status` does not reveal it because the
#  sources can already be back.
#
#  So a destructive gate now declares itself in a DISK JOURNAL that only a
#  verified restore removes, and every harness that produces EVIDENCE refuses
#  while it stands.  This is the most important of them: `--record` FREEZES an
#  answer into a golden, and a golden recorded off a sabotaged engine is a
#  wrong answer with the authority of a reference.
#
#  ONE spelling of the refusal, in Python, called from here -- a second
#  implementation in bash is how two rules drift into disagreeing.
python3 "$ROOT/bin/curate/destructive_session.py" --assert "runTests" || exit 1
cd "$ROOT"

# --- PREFLIGHT: is the build a build OF this tree? ------------------------
#
# ABORTS rather than counting a FAIL, and the distinction is the whole point.
# Every case, every golden and every gate that links a probe runs against
# build/<platform>/.  If that tree predates the sources, none of the results
# below are about the code in the repository, so producing a tally of them
# would be reporting on the wrong binary with a straight face.
#
# Paid for 2026-08-07: a rolled-back working directory left a stale build, a
# docs-only commit came back PASS 286 / FAIL 135, and the first reading blamed
# the commit.  A rebuild -- same commit, not one file edited -- gave
# PASS 432 / FAIL 0.  The suite had every symptom and named no cause.
#
# The opposite error is the reason this aborts instead of warning: a stale
# library whose goldens were recorded against it produces a CLEAN PASS, which
# looks exactly like a real one.
# GATE ZERO (2026-08-15, architectural ruling): before comparing the BUILD
# to the tree, prove the TREE against the truth.  The hosted recycler rewinds
# HEAD, restores a poisoned overlay and leaves stale remote refs -- a suite
# run on such a workspace does not waste time, it MANUFACTURES FALSE
# EVIDENCE about a tree nobody is discussing.  DEGRADED (offline) proceeds
# with the reduced claim stated; a proven-stale workspace refuses with the
# remedy printed.
if ! "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_workspace_truth.py"; then
    echo ""
    echo "ABORTED before running anything -- the WORKSPACE is not proven."
    exit 1
fi

if ! "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_build_fresh.py"; then
    printf "\nABORTED before running anything -- see above.\n"
    printf "This is not a test failure.  No case was run, and no result here "
    printf "would have described the current source.\n"
    exit 1
fi

# --- locate the binary for a case (by controlDict application; code/ -> buildCode)
binary_for() {
    local case_dir="$1"
    if [ -d "$case_dir/code" ]; then echo "BUILDCODE"; return; fi
    local app
    app=$(grep -oP '^[[:space:]]*application[[:space:]]+\K\w+' \
            "$case_dir/system/controlDict" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
    echo "${app:-choupoSolve}"
}

# --- run a case, echo its full stdout.  The case's exit code is the function's
#     RETURN status: callers run `out=$(run_case_raw ...); rc=$?`.  A first cut
#     published it in a global RUN_RC set INSIDE the $(...) -- a subshell, so the
#     assignment never reached the caller and the caller read the initial 0: the
#     exit-code guard was DEAD (column04's validate0 failure sailed through as
#     PASS).  The subshell's exit status is the one channel $(...) preserves.
run_case_raw() {
    local case_dir="$1" app="$2" rc
    if [ "$app" = BUILDCODE ]; then
        if ! "$ROOT/bin/buildCode" "$case_dir" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            echo "__BUILDFAIL__"; return 1
        fi
        "$case_dir/code/.build/choupoCase" "$case_dir" 2>&1; rc=$?
    else
        "$ROOT/$app" "$case_dir" 2>&1; rc=$?
    fi
    return "$rc"
}

# --- pull "key": value out of the line that defines `name` ({ ... } object)
get_value() {
    local out="$1" name="$2" key="$3"
    printf '%s\n' "$out" \
        | grep "\"$name\": {" | grep -m1 "\"$key\":" \
        | grep -oP "\"$key\":\s*\K-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*|\"$key\":\s*\K-?(nan|inf)" \
        | head -1
}

# --- pull "key": value out of an operationResults diagnostics object (choupoProps
#     curation ops -- estimateComponent constants, fit stats -- emit per-op
#     `{ "name": "<op>", ..., "diagnostics": {...} }`, NOT the `"<name>": {}` form
#     get_value reads, so they need their own extractor).
get_diag() {
    local out="$1" opname="$2" key="$3"
    printf '%s\n' "$out" \
        | grep "\"name\": \"$opname\"" | grep -m1 "\"diagnostics\":" \
        | grep -oP "\"$key\":\s*\K-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*|\"$key\":\s*\K-?(nan|inf)" \
        | head -1
}

# --- pull one AAD statistic out of the top-level `"validation": [...]` block
#     (choupoProps `experimental {}` overlays).
#
#     WHY IT NEEDS ITS OWN EXTRACTOR, and why that mattered.  The overlay's
#     AAD table is the ONLY place a props case says how far a model sits from
#     measured data -- the whole point of a `compare_*` case -- and it is
#     emitted NEITHER as a kpi object NOR under `operationResults[].diagnostics`.
#     So no golden row could reach it, and every AAD in the corpus was pinned
#     by nothing.  Found 2026-08-12 on compare_vle_etoh_water, whose header
#     claimed UNIFAC "T_bubble AAD ~2.6 K" against its own output's 0.407 K.
#
#     `name` is the dataset, `key` is `<model>.<property>.<stat>` -- one column
#     per axis, as for `diag`: the kind says WHERE (the validation block), the
#     name says WHICH dataset, the key says which model/property/statistic.
#     `stat` is any numeric field of the record (aadAbs, aadRelPct, nMeas,
#     nUsed, nOutOfRange, ...).  A `null` field yields nothing, so a statistic
#     the case does not publish reports as MISSING and never as agreement.
get_aad() {
    local out="$1" dataset="$2" key="$3"
    local model prop stat
    model=${key%%.*}; stat=${key##*.}
    prop=${key#*.}; prop=${prop%.*}
    printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -v ds="$dataset" -v m="$model" -v p="$prop" -v s="$stat" '
        /"dataset":/ { cur = ""; if (match($0, /"dataset": "[^"]*"/))
                       { cur = substr($0, RSTART+12, RLENGTH-13) } }
        cur == ds && index($0, "\"model\": \"" m "\"") \
                  && index($0, "\"property\": \"" p "\"") {
            if (match($0, "\"" s "\": *-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*")) {
                v = substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/^"[^"]*": */, "", v)
                print v; exit
            }
        }'
}

# --- pull one field out of the top-level `"energyClosures": [...]` ledger
#     (choupoSolve; the model-boundary energy ledger, 2026-08-09).
#
#     WHY IT NEEDS ITS OWN EXTRACTOR.  The ledger's whole contract is that
#     THREE quantities travel apart and are never collapsed -- the raw
#     imbalance, the DECLARED model-boundary step, and what REMAINS after the
#     step is credited -- and the verdict is taken on the third.  All three are
#     emitted as objects in a top-level array keyed by `"unit"`, which is
#     neither the `"<name>": {}` form get_value reads nor an
#     operationResults diagnostics object.  So no golden row could reach them:
#     found 2026-08-12, one field over from the same defect in `validation`.
#
#     What that cost: the step is credited ONLY when an INDEPENDENTLY
#     assembled package reproduces the imbalance, and if that independent
#     arithmetic drifted, `remaining_kW` would grow from 9e-9 to something
#     real and `status` would fall back -- with the suite green throughout.
#
#     `name` is the unit, `key` is any numeric field (raw_kW, step_kW,
#     remaining_kW, magnitude, normResidual).  `status`, `rule` and the two
#     world strings are WORDS and are deliberately NOT reachable here -- a
#     golden row compares numbers.  A status downgrade is caught INDIRECTLY
#     and reliably: it zeroes step_kW and pushes remaining_kW back up to raw,
#     both of which are numbers these rows pin.  Said plainly rather than
#     claimed as coverage it does not have.
get_closure() {
    local out="$1" unit="$2" key="$3"
    printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -v u="$unit" -v k="$key" '
        /"energyClosures":/ {
            n = split($0, rec, /\}, *\{/)
            for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
                if (index(rec[i], "\"unit\": \"" u "\"") == 0) continue
                #  NOTE the `||` must END the line: awk continues a statement
                #  after the operator, never before it.  Written the other way
                #  round this whole program is a syntax error, and the row
                #  reports MISSING -- which is how it was found, and is the
                #  right failure (a broken extractor must never read as an
                #  agreeing value).
                if (match(rec[i], "\"" k "\": *-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*") ||
                    match(rec[i], "\"" k "\": *-?(nan|inf)")) {
                    v = substr(rec[i], RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/^"[^"]*": */, "", v)
                    print v; exit
                }
            }
        }'
}

# --- pull one field out of the top-level `"utilityAllocation": [...]` array
#     (choupoSolve; which catalogue utility `pickForDuty` chose for a duty).
#
#     THE UTILITY NAME IS IN THE KEY, ON PURPOSE.  The decision this block
#     records is WHICH utility serves the duty -- and that is a word, which a
#     golden row cannot compare.  Putting it in the key makes the row match
#     only while that utility is the one allocated: a picker that switched
#     steamLP for steamMP does not shift a number, it makes the row report
#     MISSING, which fails and names what changed.  The consequences (kg_s,
#     eur_h, T) are pinned beside it as ordinary numbers.
#
#     WHITESPACE IN A NAME IS NORMALISED TO `_`, ON BOTH SIDES.  A golden row
#     is whitespace-delimited, so a key simply cannot carry a space -- and
#     real catalogue names do: `electricity (generated)` is the credit an
#     electrochemical cell earns, and it broke the first version of this
#     format on nineteen cases.  The substitution is applied HERE and in the
#     generator from the same rule, so the two cannot disagree.
#
#     AN UNSERVED DUTY IS PINNED TOO, and it is not an allocation.  A duty no
#     catalogue utility can meet (column07's condenser at 272.7 K) is reported
#     with `allocated: false` and a PROSE placeholder in the utility field --
#     `(none adequate)` -- which must never be pinned as though it were a
#     name.  Such an entry gets one row, `<tier>.<port>.unserved`, whose value
#     is 1: the fact being pinned is that the duty is still unserved.  If the
#     catalogue later grows a utility that can meet it, the entry flips to
#     allocated, that row reports MISSING, and the improvement is reported
#     rather than absorbed.
#
#     `name` is the unit, `key` is `<tier>.<utility>.<field>` or
#     `<tier>.<port>.unserved` (port `-` when the entry declares none).
get_utility() {
    local out="$1" unit="$2" key="$3"
    local tier util field
    tier=${key%%.*}; field=${key##*.}
    util=${key#*.}; util=${util%.*}
    printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -v u="$unit" -v t="$tier" -v w="$util" -v f="$field" '
        function norm(s) { gsub(/[ \t]+/, "_", s); return s }
        /"utilityAllocation":/ {
            n = split($0, rec, /\}, *\{/)
            for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
                if (index(rec[i], "\"unit\": \"" u "\"") == 0) continue
                if (index(rec[i], "\"tier\": \"" t "\"") == 0) continue
                alloc = (index(rec[i], "\"allocated\": true") > 0)
                if (f == "unserved") {
                    if (alloc) continue
                    p = "-"
                    if (match(rec[i], /"port": "[^"]*"/))
                        { p = substr(rec[i], RSTART+9, RLENGTH-10); if (p == "") p = "-" }
                    if (norm(p) != w) continue
                    print 1; exit
                }
                if (!alloc) continue
                if (!match(rec[i], /"utility": "[^"]*"/)) continue
                if (norm(substr(rec[i], RSTART+12, RLENGTH-13)) != w) continue
                if (match(rec[i], "\"" f "\": *-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*")) {
                    v = substr(rec[i], RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/^"[^"]*": */, "", v)
                    print v; exit
                }
            }
        }'
}

# --- dispatch by `kind`: diag -> operation diagnostics, aad -> the validation
#     overlay table, closure -> the model-boundary energy ledger, utility ->
#     the utility allocation, else kpi/stream object
get_any() {
    local out="$1" kind="$2" name="$3" key="$4"
    #  The CLAIM (published vs self-recorded) is a different column and does
    #  not reach here: it never changes where the number is read from.
    if   [ "$kind" = diag    ]; then get_diag    "$out" "$name" "$key"
    elif [ "$kind" = aad     ]; then get_aad     "$out" "$name" "$key"
    elif [ "$kind" = closure ]; then get_closure "$out" "$name" "$key"
    elif [ "$kind" = utility ]; then get_utility "$out" "$name" "$key"
    elif [ "$kind" = csv     ]; then get_csv     "${5:-}" "$name" "$key"
    else get_value "$out" "$name" "$key"; fi
}

#  csv <file> <rowIdx:colName> -- reads one cell of a case-emitted CSV (the
#  sweep drivers publish their whole answer as a CSV no JSON block carries;
#  a published number a golden cannot reach is the defect class closed three
#  times already -- 2026-08-22, the fourth).  rowIdx is the 0-based DATA row;
#  colName must match the header exactly.  A missing file, row or column
#  yields empty -> the row reports MISSING, never a silent pass.
get_csv() {
    awk -F, -v spec="$3" '
        NR==1 { n=split(spec,a,":"); ri=a[1]; cn=a[2];
                for (i=1;i<=NF;i++) if ($i==cn) ci=i; next }
        NR==ri+2 { if (ci) print $ci }' "$1/$2" 2>/dev/null
}

# --- catalogue identity + CAS audit (curation guard, NOT the hot path) ----
#  Three checks over data/standards/components/*.dat, all HARD failures:
#    1. the stored `name` field == the filename stem (the canonical key);
#    2. filename stems are unique (guaranteed by the filesystem, asserted);
#    3. no two files share a REAL CAS (sentinels 00-00-0 / n/a excluded) --
#       the duplicate-CAS twin defect (C3H8/propane, NH3/ammonia) must never
#       come back.  CAS is an audit key here, never a lookup key.
audit_catalogue() {
    local dir="$ROOT/data/standards/components" problems=0
    declare -A cas_files
    local f stem nm cas
    for f in "$dir"/*.dat; do
        [ -f "$f" ] || continue
        stem=$(basename "$f" .dat)
        nm=$(awk '/^[[:space:]]*name[[:space:]]/{v=$2; sub(/;.*/,"",v); gsub(/"/,"",v); print v; exit}' "$f")
        cas=$(awk '/^[[:space:]]*CAS[[:space:]]/{v=$2; sub(/;.*/,"",v); gsub(/"/,"",v); print v; exit}' "$f")
        if [ -n "$nm" ] && [ "$nm" != "$stem" ]; then
            printf "FAIL  %-40s  (name '%s' != filename stem)\n" "catalogue:$stem" "$nm"
            problems=$((problems+1))
        fi
        case "$cas" in
            ""|00-00-0|n/a|na|NA|none) ;;                     # sentinels: skip
            *)  if [ -n "${cas_files[$cas]:-}" ]; then
                    printf "FAIL  %-40s  (duplicate CAS %s -- also %s)\n" \
                        "catalogue:$stem" "$cas" "${cas_files[$cas]}"
                    problems=$((problems+1))
                else
                    cas_files[$cas]="$stem"
                fi ;;
        esac
    done

    # HARD: an ESTIMATE-status component must NEVER live in the frozen catalogue
    # (promoted Joback proposals belong in a case's constant/components/, not here).
    local est
    est=$(grep -liE '^[[:space:]]*status[[:space:]].*ESTIMATE' "$dir"/*.dat 2>/dev/null || true)
    if [ -n "$est" ]; then
        while IFS= read -r e; do
            [ -n "$e" ] || continue
            printf "FAIL  %-40s  (ESTIMATE-status in the FROZEN catalogue)\n" "catalogue:$(basename "$e" .dat)"
            problems=$((problems+1))
        done <<< "$est"
    fi

    # SOFT, informational: the per-value provenance split (live count, not a magic
    # constant) — the honest "N annotated / M unattributed" state.
    local total ann
    total=$(ls "$dir"/*.dat 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
    ann=$(grep -lE '^[[:space:]]*provenance' "$dir"/*.dat 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
    [ "$problems" -eq 0 ] && printf "PASS  %-40s  (identity + unique CAS; provenance %s/%s annotated)\n" "catalogue" "$ann" "$total"
    return "$problems"
}

# --- numeric compare: |a-e|/|e| <= tol, EXCEPT near zero.
#  A golden value below 1e-9 is cancellation/consumption residue, not an
#  answer: a relative band on it pins the last bits of an ODE step order or a
#  libm, and fails on a compiler flag while catching no physics.  Such a row
#  therefore asserts "this quantity is ~ZERO", compared ABSOLUTELY against
#  tol -- the same claim an exact-0 row has always made.  (Until 2026-08-22
#  the cliff sat at 1e-30: 225 rows between 1e-30 and 1e-9 carried brittle
#  relative bands, and 6 rows below it silently got the loose absolute one --
#  two semantics, chosen by which side of an undocumented constant the
#  recorded noise happened to land.  Fresh-eyes audit, finding C4/C5.)
within_tol() {
    awk -v a="$1" -v e="$2" -v t="$3" 'BEGIN{
        if (a == "" )            { exit 2 }       # missing
        ae = (e<0)?-e:e
        diff = a-e; if (diff<0) diff=-diff
        rel = (ae > 1e-9) ? diff/ae : diff
        exit (rel <= t) ? 0 : 1
    }'
}

RECORD=
if [ "${1:-}" = "--record" ]; then RECORD=1; shift; fi

#  --witnesses: the ARCHITECTURAL WITNESS TIER (tutorials/WITNESSES) -- one
#  representative case per execution class, run through the SAME per-case
#  checks as the full sweep.  Implemented by expanding the declared list into
#  the positional args, so the gate arms guarded by `$# -eq 0` are skipped
#  exactly as they are for any explicit case selection: the tier answers
#  "does each class still traverse the architecture?", not "does every gate
#  and historical case still hold?" -- that second question is the full
#  sweep's, at campaign closure.  Ladder: verification-and-validation.md.
if [ "${1:-}" = "--witnesses" ]; then
    shift
    [ "$#" -eq 0 ] || { echo "usage: runTests --witnesses  (takes no cases; the list is tutorials/WITNESSES)"; exit 2; }
    [ -f "$ROOT/tutorials/WITNESSES" ] || { echo "ERROR: tutorials/WITNESSES missing -- the witness tier is a declared list, and it is gone"; exit 1; }
    WLIST=()
    while read -r wclass wpath _; do
        case "$wclass" in ''|\#*) continue;; esac
        WLIST+=("$ROOT/$wpath")
    done < "$ROOT/tutorials/WITNESSES"
    [ "${#WLIST[@]}" -gt 0 ] || { echo "ERROR: tutorials/WITNESSES declares no witnesses"; exit 1; }
    echo "[witness tier] ${#WLIST[@]} class witness(es) from tutorials/WITNESSES (gates skipped -- this is the class-traversal check, not the full sweep)"
    set -- "${WLIST[@]}"
fi

# --- gather cases ---------------------------------------------------------
CASES=()
if [ "$#" -gt 0 ]; then
    #  An argument may be a CASE or a DIRECTORY OF CASES.  Passing
    #  `tutorials/steady/flash` used to be taken as one case, fail to find its
    #  controlDict and report a crash -- so running the corpus in slices meant
    #  expanding the paths by hand, which is exactly the friction that keeps a
    #  long sweep from being run in pieces at all.
    #
    #  This matters since the ladder was amended (2026-08-14): the FULL sweep
    #  belongs to a release TAG and is to be run in CHUNKS, because a session
    #  that loses a 45-minute run to a killed container has produced nothing.
    #  An instruction to run it in pieces is empty if pieces are awkward.
    for a in "$@"; do
        if [ -f "$a/system/controlDict" ]; then
            CASES+=("$a")
        elif [ -d "$a" ]; then
            n=0
            while IFS= read -r cd; do
                CASES+=("$(dirname "$(dirname "$cd")")"); n=$((n+1))
            done < <(find "$a" -path '*/system/controlDict' 2>/dev/null | sort)
            [ "$n" -gt 0 ] \
                && echo "[chunk] $a -> $n case(s)" \
                || { echo "ERROR: '$a' is a directory holding no case"; exit 2; }
        else
            echo "ERROR: '$a' is neither a case nor a directory"; exit 2
        fi
    done
else
    # A case is any directory holding system/controlDict, at ANY depth -- so the
    # <category>/<subclass>/<case> layout (steady/batch/props) AND the flat ctrl/
    # both work, and props/old/ (regression fodder) is swept here too.
    for cat in steady batch ctrl props electrochem; do
        [ -d "tutorials/$cat" ] || continue
        while IFS= read -r cd; do
            case_dir="$(dirname "$(dirname "$cd")")"
            # NOTE: the two opt-out markers (`.expect-nonconvergence`,
            # `.known-broken`) are NO LONGER skipped at gather time -- they are
            # kept IN the sweep and classified in the check loop so they are
            # COUNTED and NAMED in the summary (never silently dropped).  An
            # honest gate makes its exceptions visible.
            CASES+=("$case_dir")
        done < <(find "tutorials/$cat" -path '*/system/controlDict' 2>/dev/null | sort)
    done
    # plant/ is a run-only showcase EXCEPT (a) a FLAT case that ships an
    # `expected` file -- a pinned golden-master (the hda, a plain single-`units`
    # choupoSolve flowsheet) that IS checked -- and (b) any case carrying a
    # `.known-broken` marker, which is pulled in DELIBERATELY so its breakage is
    # visible in the summary rather than hidden behind the run-only exclusion.
    # A FRACTAL case (top flowsheetDict carries `children`: ChemicalPlantTutorial,
    # esterification2sector, twoSectorDemo) otherwise stays run-only -- its
    # curate-vs-simulate sector dispatch is still an open call, so it is NOT
    # auto-checked even if a stale `expected` lingers from an earlier --record.
    if [ -d "tutorials/plant" ]; then
        while IFS= read -r cd; do
            pdir="$(dirname "$(dirname "$cd")")"
            if [ -f "$pdir/.known-broken" ]; then CASES+=("$pdir"); continue; fi
            [ -f "$pdir/expected" ] || continue
            grep -q "children" "$pdir/system/flowsheetDict" 2>/dev/null && continue
            CASES+=("$pdir")
        done < <(find "tutorials/plant" -path '*/system/controlDict' 2>/dev/null | sort)
    fi
fi

# --- auto-generate an expected from a run (steady cases only) -------------
#  Pulls every numeric KPI of every unit (skipping the volatile
#  `iterations`) plus each stream's F, at a default relative tolerance.
auto_generate() {
    #  $4 -- optional destination.  Defaults to the case's own `expected`;
    #  the refresh path below writes to a scratch file instead so it can
    #  diff the generated rows against the golden it is about to keep.
    local case_dir="$1" out="$2" reltol="${3:-1e-4}" dest="${4:-}"
    [ -z "$dest" ] && dest="$case_dir/expected"
    local block kpis_sec streams_sec opres_sec valid_sec closure_sec util_sec
    block=$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed -n '/result-begin/,/result-end/p')
    [ -z "$block" ] && return 1
    streams_sec=$(printf '%s\n' "$block" | sed -n '/"streams": {/,/^  },\?$/p')
    kpis_sec=$(printf '%s\n'    "$block" | sed -n '/"kpis": {/,/^  },\?$/p')
    opres_sec=$(printf '%s\n'   "$block" | sed -n '/"operationResults": \[/,/^  \],\?$/p')
    valid_sec=$(printf '%s\n'   "$block" | sed -n '/"validation": \[/,/^  \],\?$/p')
    closure_sec=$(printf '%s\n' "$block" | grep '"energyClosures":')
    util_sec=$(printf '%s\n'    "$block" | grep '"utilityAllocation":')
    [ -z "$kpis_sec$streams_sec$opres_sec$valid_sec" ] && return 1
    {
        echo "# Reference KPIs (golden master, auto-recorded by runTests --record)."
        echo "# units: stream F is kmol/s (canonical SI, as in the result JSON); the run LOG prints kmol/h."
        echo "# kind   name      key         expected        reltol"
        # streams: F of each
        printf '%s\n' "$streams_sec" | grep -P '"\w+": \{.*"F":' | while read -r line; do
            local s f
            # head -1: a stream line has TWO ": {" (the name and the
            # "composition" sub-object) --- take the stream name only.
            s=$(printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oP '"\K\w+(?=": \{)' | head -1)
            f=$(printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oP '"F":\s*\K-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*' | head -1)
            [ -n "$s" ] && [ -n "$f" ] && printf 'stream  %-22s %-16s %-18s %s\n' "$s" "F" "$f" "$reltol"
        done
        # kpis: every numeric key per unit (skip iterations)
        printf '%s\n' "$kpis_sec" | grep -P '"\w+": \{' | while read -r line; do
            local u
            u=$(printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oP '"\K\w+(?=": \{)')
            [ -z "$u" ] && continue
            printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oP '"\w+":\s*-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*' | while read -r kv; do
                local k v
                k=$(printf '%s' "$kv" | grep -oP '^"\K\w+')
                v=$(printf '%s' "$kv" | grep -oP ':\s*\K-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*')
                [ "$k" = iterations ] && continue
                printf 'kpi     %-22s %-16s %-18s %s\n' "$u" "$k" "$v" "$reltol"
            done
        done
        # operation diagnostics: deterministic curation-op KPIs (estimateComponent
        # constants, fit stats) -- pins the estimation path's physics.
        printf '%s\n' "$opres_sec" | grep -P '"name": "\w+".*"diagnostics": \{' | while read -r line; do
            local o diags
            o=$(printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oP '"name": "\K\w+' | head -1)
            [ -z "$o" ] && continue
            diags=$(printf '%s' "$line" | grep -oP '"diagnostics": \{\K[^}]*')
            printf '%s' "$diags" | grep -oP '"\w+":\s*-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*' | while read -r kv; do
                local k v
                k=$(printf '%s' "$kv" | grep -oP '^"\K\w+')
                v=$(printf '%s' "$kv" | grep -oP ':\s*\K-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*')
                printf 'diag    %-22s %-16s %-18s %s\n' "$o" "$k" "$v" "$reltol"
            done
        done
        #  overlay AADs: how far each model sits from the MEASURED dataset.
        #  These are the numbers a `compare_*` case exists to publish, and
        #  until 2026-08-12 auto-generation walked straight past them, so the
        #  headline result of every overlay case was pinned by nothing.
        #
        #  They are SELF-RECORDED rows, never anchors, and the difference is
        #  not a formality: an AAD is a statistic DERIVED from the model and
        #  the published dataset together, so nobody published it and no
        #  primary can be quoted for it.  What it pins is that the agreement
        #  has not MOVED.  Anchoring this case means carrying a measured point
        #  from Carey & Lewis and comparing the model AT it -- a different row.
        #
        #  Only aadAbs/aadRelPct are emitted: nMeas/nUsed and the rejection
        #  counters are properties of the DATASET, not of the model, and
        #  pinning them here would spread a dataset fact over one row per
        #  model.  A dataset that shrinks shows up in every AAD anyway.
        printf '%s\n' "$valid_sec" | awk -v tol="$reltol" '
            /"dataset":/ { if (match($0, /"dataset": "[^"]*"/))
                           { ds = substr($0, RSTART+12, RLENGTH-13) } }
            /"model":/ {
                if (!match($0, /"model": "[^"]*"/)) next
                m = substr($0, RSTART+10, RLENGTH-11)
                if (!match($0, /"property": "[^"]*"/)) next
                p = substr($0, RSTART+13, RLENGTH-14)
                split("aadAbs aadRelPct", st, " ")
                for (i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
                    if (match($0, "\"" st[i] "\": *-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*")) {
                        v = substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/^"[^"]*": */, "", v)
                        printf "aad     %-22s %-16s %-18s %s\n", ds, m "." p "." st[i], v, tol
                    }
                }
            }'
        #  model-boundary energy ledger: the three quantities that must never
        #  be collapsed, for the units that actually declare a boundary.
        #
        #  ONLY the units whose status is NOT `none`.  A unit with no declared
        #  boundary reports three zeros, and pinning a zero per unit on every
        #  steady case in the corpus would rewrite every golden to say the same
        #  nothing.  What is pinned is what the ledger CLAIMED: a step it
        #  credited, and the residue it credited it against.
        #
        #  THE BAND COMES FROM AN ABSOLUTE FLOOR, not from the relative size
        #  of the quantities, and the project has already paid for the reason.
        #  A value below a MICROWATT is numerically zero for a process energy
        #  balance: it is cancellation round-off, and a 1e-4 RELATIVE band on
        #  it pins round-off -- it fails on a compiler flag while catching no
        #  physics.  That is the column13 lesson verbatim (a golden pinning a
        #  one-nanowatt re-flash duty at 1e-4 relative was pinning
        #  cancellation), applied here before it can be re-learnt.
        #
        #  Such a row is emitted at `1e0`, which asserts the ORDER OF
        #  MAGNITUDE and nothing finer, and that is still a real check: if the
        #  independent audit stopped reproducing the step, basis01's remaining
        #  would climb from 9.4e-9 to the full 2.99 kW -- eight orders of
        #  magnitude through a factor-of-two band.
        #
        #  Above the floor the default band applies, so perUnitThermo01's
        #  turbine (status `unconfirmed`: an independently computed 7.5 kW
        #  against a 536 kW imbalance, step NOT credited) pins all three of
        #  its real numbers tight.  An EXACT zero also keeps the default: with
        #  expected 0 the comparison is already absolute, and 1e-4 kW is a
        #  tenth of a watt -- tighter than the floor and correct there.
        printf '%s\n' "$closure_sec" | awk -v tol="$reltol" '
            function abs(x) { return x < 0 ? -x : x }
            {
                n = split($0, rec, /\}, *\{/)
                for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
                    if (!match(rec[i], /"status": "[^"]*"/)) continue
                    st = substr(rec[i], RSTART+11, RLENGTH-12)
                    if (st == "none") continue
                    if (!match(rec[i], /"unit": "[^"]*"/)) continue
                    u = substr(rec[i], RSTART+9, RLENGTH-10)
                    split("raw_kW step_kW remaining_kW", f, " ")
                    for (j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
                        if (!match(rec[i], "\"" f[j] "\": *-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*")) continue
                        v = substr(rec[i], RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/^"[^"]*": */, "", v)
                        roundoff = (v + 0 != 0 && abs(v + 0) < 1e-6)
                        printf "closure %-22s %-16s %-18s %s\n", u, f[j], v,
                               (roundoff ? "1e0" : tol)
                    }
                }
            }'
        #  utility allocation: which catalogue utility `pickForDuty` chose for
        #  each duty, and what that choice costs.  The chosen utility's NAME
        #  is part of the key rather than a value, so a different pick makes
        #  the row MISSING instead of shifting a number -- see get_utility.
        #
        #  `MW` is deliberately left out: it is the catalogue's molar mass,
        #  which the component records already own and
        #  check_record_self_consistency already guards.  `allocated` is not a
        #  field either -- it SELECTS which shape of row is written (see
        #  get_utility): a served duty is pinned by its utility's name and
        #  numbers, an unserved one by a single `unserved` row, and the two
        #  must never be confused, because an unserved entry's utility field
        #  is a prose placeholder rather than a name.
        printf '%s\n' "$util_sec" | awk -v tol="$reltol" '
            function norm(s) { gsub(/[ \t]+/, "_", s); return s }
            {
                n = split($0, rec, /\}, *\{/)
                for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
                    if (!match(rec[i], /"unit": "[^"]*"/))    continue
                    u = substr(rec[i], RSTART+9, RLENGTH-10)
                    if (!match(rec[i], /"tier": "[^"]*"/))    continue
                    t = substr(rec[i], RSTART+9, RLENGTH-10)
                    if (index(rec[i], "\"allocated\": true") == 0) {
                        p = "-"
                        if (match(rec[i], /"port": "[^"]*"/))
                            { p = substr(rec[i], RSTART+9, RLENGTH-10); if (p == "") p = "-" }
                        printf "utility %-22s %-16s %-18s %s\n", u,
                               t "." norm(p) ".unserved", 1, tol
                        continue
                    }
                    if (!match(rec[i], /"utility": "[^"]*"/)) continue
                    w = norm(substr(rec[i], RSTART+12, RLENGTH-13))
                    split("duty_kW T kg_s eur_h", f, " ")
                    for (j = 1; j <= 4; j++) {
                        if (!match(rec[i], "\"" f[j] "\": *-?[0-9][0-9.eE+-]*")) continue
                        v = substr(rec[i], RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/^"[^"]*": */, "", v)
                        printf "utility %-22s %-16s %-18s %s\n", u, t "." w "." f[j], v, tol
                    }
                }
            }'
    } > "$dest"
    return 0
}

# --- record mode: refresh existing expected, or auto-generate new ---------
if [ -n "$RECORD" ]; then
    for case_dir in "${CASES[@]}"; do
        # Never auto-record a golden from a DELIBERATELY-failing or KNOWN-BROKEN
        # run -- that would pin broken physics as the reference.
        if [ -f "$case_dir/.expect-nonconvergence" ] || [ -f "$case_dir/.known-broken" ]; then
            echo "skip (marked: not recordable): $case_dir"; continue
        fi
        exp="$case_dir/expected"
        app=$(binary_for "$case_dir"); out=$(run_case_raw "$case_dir" "$app")
        if [ -f "$exp" ]; then
            tmp=$(mktemp)
            while read -r line; do
                case "$line" in ''|\#*) echo "$line" >> "$tmp"; continue;; esac
                read -r kind name key exp_v tol claim _ <<< "$line"
                #  AN ANCHOR IS NEVER REFRESHED.  Its expected column is a
                #  PUBLISHED value; rewriting it with what the model happens
                #  to produce would convert a disagreement into a reference
                #  and destroy the only row in the file that can falsify the
                #  case's citation.
                if [ "${claim:-}" = anchor ]; then
                    printf '%-7s %-22s %-16s %-16s %-8s %s\n' "$kind" "$name" "$key" "$exp_v" "$tol" "$claim" >> "$tmp"
                    continue
                fi
                act=$(get_any "$out" "$kind" "$name" "$key")
                printf '%-7s %-22s %-16s %-16s %s\n' "$kind" "$name" "$key" "${act:-$exp_v}" "$tol" >> "$tmp"
            done < "$exp"
            mv "$tmp" "$exp"

            #  A REFRESH THAT CANNOT ADD A ROW LEAVES A NEW KPI UNPINNED.
            #  The loop above only rewrites the VALUE of rows that already
            #  exist, so a unit that starts publishing a new number is born
            #  checked by nothing -- and nothing anywhere says so.  That is
            #  the same defect the `aad`/`closure`/`utility` kinds were built
            #  to close one layer up, and it bit the day `conversionReactor`
            #  split its duty into `Q_reaction_kW` + `Q_sensible_kW`: both
            #  appeared in every reacting case's result JSON and in none of
            #  their goldens.
            #
            #  So: generate what a FRESH record would write, and append only
            #  the (kind, name, key) triples the file does not already carry.
            #  Existing rows are never touched here -- hand-written rows,
            #  anchors and per-row tolerances all survive, because this
            #  appends and does not merge.
            gen=$(mktemp)
            if auto_generate "$case_dir" "$out" "1e-4" "$gen"; then
                added=0
                while read -r gkind gname gkey gval gtol _; do
                    case "$gkind" in kpi|stream|diag|aad|closure|utility) ;; *) continue;; esac
                    if ! awk -v k="$gkind" -v n="$gname" -v y="$gkey" \
                         '$1==k && $2==n && $3==y {found=1} END{exit !found}' "$exp"; then
                        printf '%-7s %-22s %-16s %-16s %s\n' \
                            "$gkind" "$gname" "$gkey" "$gval" "$gtol" >> "$exp"
                        added=$((added+1))
                    fi
                done < "$gen"
                [ "$added" -gt 0 ] \
                    && echo "  + $added new row(s) appended (keys the golden did not carry)"
            fi
            rm -f "$gen"
            echo "refreshed: $exp"
        elif auto_generate "$case_dir" "$out"; then
            echo "generated: $exp  ($(grep -cE '^(kpi|stream|diag)' "$exp") refs)"
        else
            echo "skip (no kpis/streams block): $case_dir"
        fi
    done
    exit 0
fi

# --- check mode -----------------------------------------------------------
pass=0; fail=0; known_broken=0; expected_fail=0
KB_NAMES=(); EF_NAMES=()

# Catalogue identity + CAS audit runs once on the FULL sweep (it is a property
# of the whole standard catalogue, not of any one case).
if [ "$#" -eq 0 ]; then
    audit_catalogue; nprob=$?
    if [ "$nprob" -eq 0 ]; then pass=$((pass+1)); else fail=$((fail+nprob)); fi
    # Doctrine gate (forum #91/#95): user-facing teaching surfaces must not
    # teach the retired grammars (streams{}, thermoPackage/propertyPackage
    # filenames, fitBinaryPair) nor name competitors.  Full-sweep only, like
    # the catalogue audit -- a property of the whole tree, not of one case.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_doctrine.py" > /tmp/doctrine.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "doctrine-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/doctrine.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (forbidden teaching -- run bin/curate/check_doctrine.py)\n" "doctrine-gate"
        cat /tmp/doctrine.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/doctrine.$$

    #  README CAPABILITY MATRIX vs the engine's registries (2026-08-10).  An
    #  outside reviewer found the public README claiming four binaries, SRK
    #  and Peng-Robinson in its prose while its own tables still said three
    #  binaries, `Equation of state: idealGas` and `solid (stub)`.  Six rows
    #  were stale.  Their objection is the reason this runs on every sweep:
    #  "if the EOS line is stale, how many other capability claims are stale
    #  too?"  General prose staleness stays UNGATED by ruling; a capability
    #  TABLE is the tractable exception -- fixed rows, machine-readable keys.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_readme_capabilities.py" > /tmp/readmecap.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "readme-capabilities" "$(head -1 /tmp/readmecap.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (stale public claim -- run bin/curate/check_readme_capabilities.py)\n" "readme-capabilities"
        cat /tmp/readmecap.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/readmecap.$$

    #  The `material { ... }` wrapper (slice A of Vitor's aqueous-analysis
    #  ruling, 2026-08-10): one rule for every canonical form, and EXACTLY ONE
    #  authoritative material-input form per inlet -- a wrapper beside a
    #  top-level form is refused rather than resolved by an undeclared
    #  precedence.  The round trip is witnessed, not asserted.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_material_wrapper.py" > /tmp/matwrap.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "material-wrapper" "$(head -1 /tmp/matwrap.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_material_wrapper.py)\n" "material-wrapper"
        cat /tmp/matwrap.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/matwrap.$$

    #  gamma_pm, the MEASURABLE activity coefficient (2026-08-10).  Every
    #  gamma the speciation published before this was a single-ion one, which
    #  is not measurable, so a case comparing against a published table
    #  combined the ions BY HAND in a header comment -- and that hand
    #  calculation went stale unnoticed on the four divalent seawater salts.
    #  The gate recomputes every published gamma_pm from the run's own
    #  per-ion gammas and the species charges, and requires the derived
    #  stoichiometry to be announced.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_mean_ionic.py" > /tmp/meanionic.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "mean-ionic-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/meanionic.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_mean_ionic.py)\n" "mean-ionic-gate"
        cat /tmp/meanionic.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/meanionic.$$

    #  The witness tier's declared list stays resolvable (2026-08-10): every
    #  line an existing case with a golden, classes unique.  The tier itself
    #  is `runTests --witnesses`; this arm only keeps its declaration honest.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_witness_tier.py" > /tmp/witness.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "witness-tier-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/witness.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_witness_tier.py)\n" "witness-tier-gate"
        cat /tmp/witness.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/witness.$$

    #  The OPERATIONAL MEMORY stays live (2026-08-10, Vitor's ruling): the
    #  ownership index resolves (paths/gates/witness classes exist), and the
    #  two generated artefacts -- codeMap.json (classes, the compiler's own
    #  include graph, the factory registries) and caseManifest.json (roles +
    #  reverse indices) -- match the tree they claim to describe.  A stale
    #  map sends the impact brief to the wrong blast radius with confidence,
    #  which is worse than no map.  The fourth arm is the equilibriumState
    #  cross-reference (same day's ruling): a reference must never outlive
    #  the canonical state it names.
    for om_gate in "ownership-index-gate check_ownership_index.py" \
                   "code-map-gate code_map.py --check" \
                   "case-manifest-gate case_manifest.py --check" \
                   "equilibrium-state-ref-gate check_equilibrium_state_ref.py" \
                   "aqueous-qualifier-gate check_aqueous_qualifier.py" \
                   "derived-density-gate check_derived_density.py" \
                   "volumetric-rung1-gate check_volumetric_rung1.py" \
                   "models-catalogue-gate check_models_catalogue.py" \
                   "evidence-partition-gate check_evidence_partition.py" \
                   "thermoml-index-gate check_thermoml_index.py"; do
        read -r om_name om_script om_arg <<< "$om_gate"
        if "$ROOT/bin/curate/$om_script" ${om_arg:-} > /tmp/opmem.$$ 2>&1; then
            printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "$om_name" "$(head -1 /tmp/opmem.$$)"
            pass=$((pass+1))
        else
            printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/%s %s)\n" "$om_name" "$om_script" "${om_arg:-}"
            cat /tmp/opmem.$$
            fail=$((fail+1))
        fi
        rm -f /tmp/opmem.$$
    done

    #  GUI TYPECHECK -- because a green suite did not mean the site would
    #  BUILD (2026-08-10).  vitest does not typecheck: a test file importing
    #  a type from a module that only re-imports it passed `npm test` and was
    #  rejected by `tsc`, which bin/buildSite and the publish workflow both
    #  run.  publish-site therefore failed on every commit for hours while
    #  the suite reported green, and the Mass Balance plot fix riding those
    #  commits never reached the site -- the live build stayed on the
    #  previous publish, still double-counting the crystal.  CI was the only
    #  thing that could catch it, and it catches it AFTER main.  This arm
    #  moves the check to before the push.
    #
    #  A CHECK THAT CANNOT RUN MUST NOT PASS: with no gui/node_modules (a
    #  C++-only checkout, which is legitimate) it reports SKIPPED and is
    #  counted as NEITHER pass nor fail -- the green line never claims
    #  coverage the run did not have.
    if [ -d "$ROOT/gui/node_modules" ]; then
        if (cd "$ROOT/gui" && npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit) > /tmp/guitsc.$$ 2>&1; then
            printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "gui-typecheck" "tsc --noEmit clean over gui/src + gui/tests -- the site can build"
            pass=$((pass+1))
        else
            printf "FAIL  %-40s  (tsc rejects the GUI -- the site publish WILL fail)\n" "gui-typecheck"
            head -12 /tmp/guitsc.$$
            fail=$((fail+1))
        fi
        rm -f /tmp/guitsc.$$
    else
        printf "SKIP  %-40s  (gui/node_modules absent -- run npm install in gui/ to cover the site build; NOT counted as a pass)\n" "gui-typecheck"
    fi

    #  EDUTOOLS ON THE LANDING (2026-08-17, docs/design/modes-and-views-in-the-
    #  top-row.md 4a).  The landing advertises the classical-method tools, and
    #  its list must BE the app's live registry -- both directions.  The ruling
    #  named the failure mode this arm exists to catch: a thirteenth tool
    #  appears in the app and not on the landing, or the reverse, and nothing
    #  says so.  Like gui-typecheck it needs gui/node_modules (the registry is
    #  TypeScript and is EVALUATED, not parsed), so exit 2 means CANNOT RUN and
    #  is counted as neither pass nor fail.
    "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_method_tools.py" > /tmp/mtools.$$ 2>&1
    mt_rc=$?
    if [ "$mt_rc" = 0 ]; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "method-tools-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/mtools.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    elif [ "$mt_rc" = 2 ]; then
        printf "SKIP  %-40s  (%s; NOT counted as a pass)\n" "method-tools-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/mtools.$$)"
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_method_tools.py)\n" "method-tools-gate"
        cat /tmp/mtools.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/mtools.$$

    # Tree gate (Migration 6): retired data/standards homes must never come
    # back -- neither as src path-builders nor as resurrected directories.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_tree_gate.py" > /tmp/treegate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "tree-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/treegate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (retired home referenced -- run bin/curate/check_tree_gate.py)\n" "tree-gate"
        cat /tmp/treegate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/treegate.$$

    # Lithium route gate (Codex audit, M6): the golden pins the RESULT; this
    # pins the ROUTE -- the EXTRACTION sector's 5 tuned pairs must resolve
    # from the sealed snapshot (never idealDefault) and the organic phase
    # must stay loaded (x_LiCl) and dry (x_water).
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_lithium_routes.py" > /tmp/ligate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "lithium-route-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/ligate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (route degraded -- run bin/curate/check_lithium_routes.py)\n" "lithium-route-gate"
        cat /tmp/ligate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/ligate.$$

    # Species-ontology gate (Codex P0-semantico, 2026-07-18): flat modelSpecies
    # files, formula-typed, neutrals (O2/N2 aq) never mislabelled as ions.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_species_ontology.py" > /tmp/spgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "species-ontology-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/spgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_species_ontology.py)\n" "species-ontology-gate"
        cat /tmp/spgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/spgate.$$

    # Retired-name gate (Codex tutorial-audit P2, 2026-07-18): no tutorial
    # SOURCE may cite a retired architecture name -- a green case with a false
    # comment teaches the wrong layout.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_retired_names.py" > /tmp/retgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "retired-name-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/retgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_retired_names.py)\n" "retired-name-gate"
        cat /tmp/retgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/retgate.$$

    # v2 negative-parity gate (Codex 2026-07-18, the translateV2 retirement):
    # every refusal the retired scaffold enforced must survive in the NATIVE
    # reader/dispatch -- a bad system silently ACCEPTED fails the release.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_v2_refusals.py" > /tmp/neggate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "v2-negative-parity-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/neggate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_v2_refusals.py)\n" "v2-negative-parity-gate"
        cat /tmp/neggate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/neggate.$$

    # Composite-report gate: a composite/named-edge case's reports must carry
    # the REAL boundary and a non-empty global balance (roles derive from the
    # flattened solver topology, never from walking the raw flowsheetDict).
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_composite_reports.py" > /tmp/comprep.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "composite-report-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/comprep.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_composite_reports.py)\n" "composite-report-gate"
        cat /tmp/comprep.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/comprep.$$

    # Stream-face gate: the aggregated snapshot grammar (streamFaces/faces{})
    # is what every writer AND the official migrator produce -- the retired
    # `streams` spelling must not be resurrectable.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_stream_faces.py" > /tmp/sfgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "stream-face-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/sfgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_stream_faces.py)\n" "stream-face-gate"
        cat /tmp/sfgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/sfgate.$$

    # Element-composition gate: THE shared formula parser decomposes the
    # ratified grammar (groups, hydrates, charges, isotopes) and refuses the
    # rest loudly -- every balance (batch/steady/ctrl) rides on it.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_element_composition.py" > /tmp/elgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "element-composition-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/elgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_element_composition.py)\n" "element-composition-gate"
        cat /tmp/elgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/elgate.$$

    # Species-identity gate: chemical truth lives in explicit fields, never in
    # the key.  Prospective (a NEW opaque name is refused; the ~59 historical
    # keys are pinned, not blessed), and it WATCHES the six identities that are
    # still declared twice -- that vigilance is precisely what lets the ratified
    # schema-v2 campaign wait instead of being rushed.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_species_identity.py" > /tmp/sidgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "species-identity-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/sidgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_species_identity.py)\n" "species-identity-gate"
        cat /tmp/sidgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/sidgate.$$

    # Navigation-ontology gate: metadata/aqueous-navigation.dat is a VIEW input,
    # never a runtime one.  Proves no phantom species, complete family coverage,
    # the mediators declared (without them family propagation collapses every
    # acid-base reaction into `water`), and NO solver reading the file -- which
    # is what keeps a taxonomy edit from ever drifting a sealed case.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_aqueous_navigation.py" > /tmp/navgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "aqueous-navigation-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/navgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_aqueous_navigation.py)\n" "aqueous-navigation-gate"
        cat /tmp/navgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/navgate.$$

    # Aqueous-graph gate: the family views must keep coming out of the ENGINE's
    # own record reader (one parser, never a Python re-implementation), and the
    # graph must stay finding-free.  The assertion that matters is the last one:
    # reactions belonging to MORE THAN ONE family are what a view can do and a
    # directory layout cannot -- if that count ever drops to zero, the bipartite
    # argument behind this design has silently been lost.
    if "$ROOT/choupoProps" --aqueous-graph /tmp/aqg.$$.json > /tmp/aqg.$$ 2>&1 \
       && python3 - /tmp/aqg.$$.json <<'PYEOF' >> /tmp/aqg.$$ 2>&1
import json, sys
d = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))
multi = [r for r in d["reactions"] if len(r["families"]) > 1]
assert d["species"],   "no species in the graph"
assert d["reactions"], "no reactions in the graph"
assert not d["findings"], "graph findings: %s" % d["findings"]
assert multi, "no reaction belongs to two families -- the view has collapsed"
print("aqueous graph: %d species, %d equilibria, %d parameters, "
      "%d reactions in 2+ families" % (len(d["species"]), len(d["reactions"]),
                                       len(d["parameters"]), len(multi)))
PYEOF
    then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "aqueous-graph-gate" "$(tail -1 /tmp/aqg.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run choupoProps --aqueous-graph)\n" "aqueous-graph-gate"
        cat /tmp/aqg.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/aqg.$$ /tmp/aqg.$$.json

    # Unit-chemistry gate: a unit op answers a PROCESS question -- which
    # activity model evaluates the aqueous phase, and which solid phases may
    # precipitate, are the declared system's, and the unit reads them.  The
    # corpus grew the opposite habit because the grammar had no slot for a
    # liquid-only electrolyte declaration; the slot exists now, the hidden
    # defaults are gone, and this keeps them gone.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_no_unit_chemistry.py" > /tmp/ucgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "unit-chemistry-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/ucgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_no_unit_chemistry.py)\n" "unit-chemistry-gate"
        cat /tmp/ucgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/ucgate.$$

    # Typed-identifier gate: ComponentId/SpeciesId/SolidId are strong types and
    # the compiler forbids the implicit crossing; this refuses the shortcut a
    # compiler cannot see -- laundering a component name into species space
    # through .name()/.key -- and proves every bridge-unit case declares its
    # component->species mappings.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_typed_identifiers.py" > /tmp/tidgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "typed-identifier-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/tidgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_typed_identifiers.py)\n" "typed-identifier-gate"
        cat /tmp/tidgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/tidgate.$$

    # aq-disambiguation gate: while RECORD identifiers are bare strings, a
    # derived neutral aqueous species homonymous with a component must carry
    # `aq` -- six such collisions exist today (CaCO3, CO2, NH3, ...).  The
    # suffix is a functional disambiguator, not decoration; its removal is
    # the F2 campaign, one closed transaction, never piecemeal.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_aq_disambiguation.py" > /tmp/aqgate.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "aq-disambiguation-gate" "$(head -1 /tmp/aqgate.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_aq_disambiguation.py)\n" "aq-disambiguation-gate"
        cat /tmp/aqgate.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/aqgate.$$

    # Phase-speciation gate: the aqueous speciation must NAME the phase it
    # decomposes.  It used to sit at the top level and be checked against the
    # OVERALL material -- every phase -- while the ions live in the aqueous
    # phase and nowhere else.  flash17 passed that to 2e-12 only because its
    # organic admits no acid; flash16 passed it by counting solid calcite as a
    # dissolved species.  The three structural refusals survive the fix being
    # reverted (verified by sabotage), so case (d) is the one that guards it.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_phase_speciation.py" > /tmp/phspec.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "phase-speciation-gate" "$(tail -1 /tmp/phspec.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_phase_speciation.py)\n" "phase-speciation-gate"
        cat /tmp/phspec.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/phspec.$$

    # Both-bases gate: where the package resolves ions, EVERY stream shows
    # both -- the inlet included.  The post-solve speciation pass sat inside
    # `if (hasReactiveEquilibrium())`, so every Pitzer/eNRTL brine in the
    # corpus reported its apparent salt and not one ion while the reactive
    # flashes reported both, and nothing failed because the corpus only ever
    # asserted the block where it already existed.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_both_bases.py" > /tmp/bothbases.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "both-bases-gate" "$(tail -1 /tmp/bothbases.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_both_bases.py)\n" "both-bases-gate"
        cat /tmp/bothbases.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/bothbases.$$

    # Stream-transport closure: ONE STREAM, ONE SEMANTICS (ruled 2026-08-08).
    # The result JSON's F/composition/H are the OVERALL material, equal to
    # converged/<stream> component by component, whose phases{} blocks sum
    # back to it; H_kW = F*H as one pair.  The JSON leaked the engine's
    # fluid-only internals for months and the GUI exposed it on flash19.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_stream_transport_closure.py" > /tmp/strclose.$$ 2>&1; then
        printf "PASS  %-40s  (%s)\n" "stream-transport-gate" "$(tail -1 /tmp/strclose.$$)"
        pass=$((pass+1))
    else
        printf "FAIL  %-40s  (run bin/curate/check_stream_transport_closure.py)\n" "stream-transport-gate"
        cat /tmp/strclose.$$
        fail=$((fail+1))
    fi
    rm -f /tmp/strclose.$$

    # Solute-basis gate: a single-salt electrolyte model answers about its
    # own salt, and the run names what else is dissolved.  Every failure it
    # guards produced a plausible number -- the boiling-point rise of a
    # liquid that does not exist, a supersaturation whose numerator and
    # denominator were on different bases -- and the corpus had no case that
    # could see any of them until evaporator09, crystalliser12 and batch14.
    if "$ROOT/bin/curate/check_solute_basis.py" > /tmp/solutebasis.$$ 2>&1; then
